/* Pathology 16: Skin, endocrine, bone, soft tissue, CNS */
Z("path16",0,"p",["Squamous cell carcinoma of skin is a cancer of the flat keratin-forming cells of the epidermis, mostly on sun-exposed skin of older people.","Sunlight's UV rays act like tiny hammers chipping at DNA until skin cells lose control and invade downward.",["Risk: UV light (most important), arsenic, tar, chronic ulcers and scars (Marjolin ulcer), immunosuppression, HPV, xeroderma pigmentosum.","Precursor: actinic keratosis; in situ form = Bowen disease.","Histology: invasive nests of atypical squamous cells with keratin pearls and intercellular bridges.","Grows locally; metastasises more than BCC, less than melanoma."],"SCC = 'Sun, Scars, keratin pearls'."],
"Squamous cell carcinoma of the skin is the second most common skin cancer, arising in sun-exposed sites of older individuals. Risk factors: UV radiation (TP53 mutations), fair skin, industrial carcinogens (tars, oils, arsenic), chronic ulcers, burn scars and draining sinuses (Marjolin ulcer), ionising radiation, immunosuppression (transplant recipients), HPV and xeroderma pigmentosum (defective DNA repair). Precursor lesions: actinic keratosis and carcinoma in situ (Bowen disease). Clinically a scaly, indurated, ulcerated nodule. Microscopy: invasive nests and cords of atypical squamous cells extending into dermis, with varying differentiation — keratin pearls, dyskeratosis and intercellular bridges in well-differentiated tumours. Metastasis to regional nodes is uncommon (< 5%) except in tumours arising in scars, lip, ear and in immunosuppressed patients.",
[["Etiology",["UV-B → pyrimidine dimers → TP53 mutation","Chemicals: arsenic, tar, soot (chimney sweep)","Chronic injury: burn scars, ulcers, sinuses, lupus vulgaris","Immunosuppression, HPV, xeroderma pigmentosum"]],
["Morphology",["Gross: indurated nodule or plaque, scaly, may ulcerate with everted edges","Micro: dysplasia through full thickness (in situ); invasive irregular nests; keratin pearls, intercellular bridges, mitoses"]],
["SCC vs BCC",[["Feature","SCC","BCC"],["Cell","Keratinocyte","Basal cell"],["Precursor","Actinic keratosis","None"],["Site","Lower lip, hands, ears","Upper face (above line mouth–ear)"],["Histology","Keratin pearls","Peripheral palisading, retraction"],["Metastasis","Occasional","Very rare"]]]],
{c:"Cutaneous SCC is largely preventable by sun protection; early excision is curative in most cases."});

Z("path16",1,"p",["Basal cell carcinoma is the commonest skin cancer — slow-growing, locally destructive, almost never metastasises; often called 'rodent ulcer'.","Like a rodent slowly gnawing deeper and wider into the face but rarely travelling elsewhere.",["Sun-exposed upper face (inner canthus, nose) of older fair-skinned people.","Pearly papule with telangiectasia; central ulcer with rolled edges.","Histology: nests of basaloid cells with peripheral palisading and retraction clefts.","Linked to Hedgehog pathway (PTCH1 mutation; Gorlin syndrome)."],"BCC = 'Pearly, Palisading, Practically never metastasises'."],
"Basal cell carcinoma is the most common invasive cancer in humans, a slow-growing, locally aggressive tumour arising from basal cells of the epidermis and follicular epithelium in hair-bearing, sun-exposed skin, especially the face above a line joining the angle of the mouth to the ear lobe. Risk factors: chronic UV exposure, fair skin, immunosuppression, arsenic, xeroderma pigmentosum and naevoid BCC (Gorlin) syndrome (PTCH1 mutation in Hedgehog pathway). Clinically: pearly papule with telangiectatic vessels, which may ulcerate centrally with rolled edges (rodent ulcer); pigmented variants exist. Microscopy: nests of basaloid cells with scant cytoplasm and hyperchromatic nuclei, peripheral palisading, stromal retraction (clefts) and mucinous stroma. Patterns: nodular, superficial, morphoeic (infiltrative), pigmented. Metastasis is extremely rare; local invasion can destroy cartilage and bone. Treated by excision or Mohs surgery.",
[["Morphology",["Gross: pearly papule, telangiectasia, central ulcer with rolled edges","Micro: basaloid cell nests from epidermis; peripheral palisading; retraction artefact; mucoid stroma","Types: nodular (commonest), superficial multifocal, morphoeic, pigmented"]],
["Pathogenesis",["UV-induced mutations","Hedgehog pathway activation: PTCH1 loss, SMO activation","p53 mutations"]],
["Clinical behaviour",["Slow growth, local destruction (rodent ulcer)","Metastasis < 0.1%","Excision, Mohs micrographic surgery; vismodegib for advanced"]]],
{c:"BCC is common, slow-growing and almost never fatal when treated early, but neglected lesions can cause major local destruction."});

Z("path16",2,"p",["Malignant melanoma is a cancer of pigment cells (melanocytes); it is the most dangerous skin cancer because it spreads early.","Normal moles are calm pigment clusters; melanoma is a pigment cell that breaks away and spreads like ink through blotting paper.",["Risk: intense intermittent sun exposure, fair skin, many/dysplastic naevi, family history (CDKN2A), BRAF mutation.","ABCDE warning signs: Asymmetry, Border irregular, Colour variation, Diameter > 6 mm, Evolving.","Radial growth phase then vertical growth phase (metastatic potential).","Breslow thickness is the most important prognostic factor; markers S-100, HMB-45, Melan-A."],"Melanoma 'ABCDE' and Breslow depth decides prognosis."],
"Malignant melanoma is a malignant tumour of melanocytes arising in skin, and less commonly in eye, mucosa and meninges. Risk factors: UV exposure (especially intermittent sunburns in childhood), fair skin, large number of naevi, dysplastic naevus syndrome, family history (CDKN2A), immunosuppression, xeroderma pigmentosum. Common mutations: BRAF V600E, NRAS, CDKN2A. Clinical warning signs (ABCDE): asymmetry, irregular border, variegated colour, diameter > 6 mm, evolution, plus itching or bleeding. Growth phases: radial (horizontal intraepidermal and superficial dermal spread, little metastatic risk) and vertical (invasion into deeper dermis as an expanding nodule with metastatic potential). Types: superficial spreading (commonest), nodular, lentigo maligna, acral lentiginous (commonest in Indians and dark-skinned people — palms, soles, nail beds). Microscopy: atypical melanocytes with large nuclei, prominent eosinophilic nucleoli, pagetoid spread; S-100, HMB-45, Melan-A positive. Prognosis depends on Breslow thickness, ulceration, mitotic rate and nodal status. Metastasises widely via lymph and blood.",
[["Types",[["Type","Features"],["Superficial spreading","Commonest in fair skin; long radial phase"],["Nodular","Vertical growth from start; worst"],["Lentigo maligna","Elderly, sun-damaged face; slow"],["Acral lentiginous","Palms, soles, nails; commonest in Indians"]]],
["Prognostic factors",["Breslow thickness (mm) — most important","Ulceration","Mitotic rate","Lymph node involvement (sentinel node)","Clark level (anatomical depth, older)"]]],
{c:"Early recognition of ABCDE changes in a pigmented lesion and excision while thin give excellent cure rates; advanced melanoma is treated with BRAF inhibitors and immunotherapy."});

Z("path16",3,"p",["Hashimoto's thyroiditis is an autoimmune disease where the immune system gradually destroys the thyroid, the commonest cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-sufficient areas.","Immune soldiers set up camp inside the thyroid, slowly dismantling the hormone factory.",["Middle-aged women; painless diffuse goitre.","Antibodies: anti-thyroid peroxidase (anti-TPO), anti-thyroglobulin.","Histology: lymphocytic infiltrate with germinal centres, Hürthle (oncocytic) cells, atrophic follicles.","Increased risk of B-cell (MALT) lymphoma."],"Hashimoto = 'Hürthle cells + Germinal centres + Hypothyroid'."],
"Hashimoto thyroiditis (chronic lymphocytic thyroiditis) is an autoimmune disease causing gradual thyroid failure, most common in women aged 45–65. It results from breakdown of self-tolerance with CD8 cytotoxic T cell–mediated killing, cytokine (IFN-γ) injury and antibody-dependent cytotoxicity; antibodies to thyroid peroxidase and thyroglobulin are present; HLA-DR3/DR5 and CTLA4 polymorphisms predispose. The thyroid is diffusely and symmetrically enlarged, pale and firm. Microscopy: dense infiltrate of lymphocytes and plasma cells with well-formed germinal centres, atrophic follicles lined by Hürthle cells (abundant eosinophilic granular cytoplasm), and variable fibrosis. Initially may cause transient hyperthyroidism (hashitoxicosis), then hypothyroidism. Associated with other autoimmune diseases and with increased risk of extranodal marginal zone B-cell lymphoma.",
[["Pathogenesis",["Loss of tolerance to thyroid antigens","CD8 T cells kill thyrocytes; Th1 cytokines","Anti-TPO, anti-Tg antibodies (ADCC)","Death receptor (Fas) mediated apoptosis"]],
["Morphology",["Diffuse symmetrical goitre, pale cut surface","Lymphoid follicles with germinal centres","Hürthle cell metaplasia, small atrophic follicles, fibrosis"]],
["Lab",["↑ TSH, ↓ free T4","Anti-TPO, anti-thyroglobulin positive","FNAC: lymphocytes and Hürthle cells"]]],
{c:"Hashimoto thyroiditis is the commonest cause of hypothyroidism in iodine-sufficient regions; lifelong thyroxine replacement is the treatment."});

Z("path16",4,"p",["Graves' disease is an autoimmune disease where antibodies switch on the thyroid continuously, causing hyperthyroidism, diffuse goitre and eye changes.","The antibody is a fake key that keeps turning the thyroid's 'on' switch (TSH receptor).",["Commonest cause of hyperthyroidism; young women.","TSH receptor-stimulating antibody (TSI).","Triad: hyperthyroidism with diffuse goitre, ophthalmopathy (exophthalmos), pretibial myxoedema.","Histology: tall columnar cells with papillary infoldings, scalloped pale colloid, lymphoid infiltrate."],"Graves' triad: 'Goitre, Glare (eyes), Grows on shins (myxoedema)'."],
"Graves disease is the most common cause of endogenous hyperthyroidism, affecting women (7:1) aged 20–40. Autoantibodies against the TSH receptor (thyroid-stimulating immunoglobulin) mimic TSH, stimulating hormone synthesis and gland growth; TSH is suppressed. Infiltrative ophthalmopathy results from T-cell–mediated inflammation, fibroblast activation and glycosaminoglycan deposition in orbital tissues (TSH receptor on orbital fibroblasts); infiltrative dermopathy (pretibial myxoedema) occurs similarly. The thyroid is symmetrically enlarged, soft and vascular (bruit). Microscopy: hyperplastic tall columnar follicular cells with papillae projecting into lumens, pale scalloped colloid, and lymphoid infiltrates. Clinical: weight loss, heat intolerance, tremor, palpitations, tachycardia, atrial fibrillation, exophthalmos, lid lag. Lab: ↓ TSH, ↑ T3/T4, TRAb positive, increased diffuse radioiodine uptake.",
[["Pathogenesis",["TSH receptor-stimulating antibody (TSI) → ↑ T3/T4 and goitre","Orbital fibroblasts + T cells → oedema, GAGs, fibrosis → exophthalmos","Genetic: HLA-DR3, CTLA4, PTPN22"]],
["Morphology",["Diffuse symmetric enlargement, beefy red cut surface","Tall columnar cells, papillary infoldings, scalloped colloid","Lymphoid aggregates"]],
["Graves vs toxic multinodular goitre",[["Feature","Graves","Toxic MNG"],["Age","Young","Elderly"],["Goitre","Diffuse","Nodular"],["Eye signs","Present","Absent"],["Antibodies","TRAb +","Negative"],["Scan","Diffuse uptake","Hot nodules"]]]],
{c:"Graves disease is an antibody-mediated stimulation of the thyroid; treatment uses antithyroid drugs, radioiodine or surgery, while eye disease may need separate care."});

Z("path16",5,"p",["A goitre is any enlargement of the thyroid; colloid (diffuse) goitre and multinodular goitre mostly result from iodine deficiency.","When iodine is scarce, the thyroid works harder (TSH pushes it) and grows bigger to try to make enough hormone.",["Endemic goitre: iodine deficiency (Himalayan belt, sub-Himalayan India).","Diffuse nontoxic (colloid) goitre: hyperplastic stage → colloid involution stage.","Multinodular goitre: repeated hyperplasia and involution → nodules, haemorrhage, fibrosis, calcification, cysts.","Most patients euthyroid; can become toxic (Plummer) or compress trachea."],"Goitre = 'iodine low → TSH high → thyroid grows'."],
"Goitre is enlargement of the thyroid gland. Diffuse nontoxic (simple, colloid) goitre and multinodular goitre usually reflect impaired thyroid hormone synthesis, most often due to dietary iodine deficiency (endemic goitre in mountainous regions), goitrogens (cabbage, cassava), or hereditary enzyme defects (sporadic). Reduced hormone output raises TSH, causing hypertrophy and hyperplasia. Diffuse goitre passes through a hyperplastic stage (crowded columnar cells, little colloid) and a colloid involution stage (large distended follicles full of colloid, flattened epithelium). With time, recurrent episodes produce multinodular goitre: massive, asymmetric, multinodular glands with haemorrhage, fibrosis, calcification and cystic change. Most patients are euthyroid; complications include mass effects (dysphagia, airway obstruction, SVC compression), toxic change (Plummer syndrome), and cosmetic disfigurement. Prevention: iodised salt.",
[["Stages",[["Stage","Gross","Micro"],["Hyperplastic","Diffuse symmetrical, mild","Columnar cells, scant colloid"],["Colloid involution","Diffuse, glistening brown","Large follicles, abundant colloid, flat cells"],["Multinodular","Asymmetric, nodular, cysts, haemorrhage, calcification","Follicles of varying size, fibrosis, haemosiderin"]]],
["Causes",["Iodine deficiency (endemic)","Goitrogens: cabbage, cauliflower, cassava, drugs (lithium, PAS)","Dyshormonogenesis (sporadic)","Physiological: puberty, pregnancy"]]],
{c:"Endemic goitre is preventable with universal salt iodisation; large multinodular goitres may need surgery for pressure symptoms or suspected malignancy."});

Z("path16",6,"p",["Diabetes mellitus is a chronic disorder of high blood sugar due to lack of insulin (type 1) or resistance to insulin with inadequate secretion (type 2), damaging blood vessels and nerves over time.","Insulin is the key that opens cells for glucose; in type 1 the key is lost, in type 2 the locks are rusty and the key-maker gets tired.",["Diagnosis: fasting glucose ≥ 126 mg/dL, 2-h OGTT ≥ 200, HbA1c ≥ 6.5%, random ≥ 200 with symptoms.","Type 1: autoimmune β-cell destruction, young, ketoacidosis, insulitis.","Type 2: insulin resistance + β-cell dysfunction, obesity, amyloid in islets.","Complications: macrovascular (MI, stroke, gangrene) and microvascular (retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy)."],"Complications 'ROAD': Retinopathy, Organs (kidney), Atherosclerosis, nerve Damage."],
"Diabetes mellitus is a group of metabolic disorders sharing hyperglycaemia, resulting from defects in insulin secretion, insulin action, or both. Diagnosis (ADA): fasting plasma glucose ≥ 126 mg/dL, 2-hour OGTT ≥ 200 mg/dL, HbA1c ≥ 6.5%, or random glucose ≥ 200 mg/dL with classic symptoms. Classification: type 1 (autoimmune β-cell destruction, absolute insulin deficiency; HLA-DR3/DR4; islet autoantibodies), type 2 (peripheral insulin resistance with inadequate β-cell compensation; obesity, genetics), monogenic forms (MODY, neonatal), gestational diabetes and secondary diabetes (pancreatitis, haemochromatosis, Cushing, drugs). Pathology: type 1 shows insulitis and β-cell loss; type 2 shows islet amyloid. Chronic hyperglycaemia damages tissues by advanced glycation end products, protein kinase C activation, polyol pathway (sorbitol) and hexosamine pathway, leading to macrovascular disease (accelerated atherosclerosis — MI, stroke, peripheral gangrene; hyaline arteriolosclerosis) and microvascular disease (retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy). Acute complications: diabetic ketoacidosis (type 1) and hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state (type 2), hypoglycaemia. Infections are frequent.",
[["Classification",[["Type","Mechanism","Features"],["Type 1 (5–10%)","Autoimmune β-cell destruction","Children/young, thin, ketosis-prone, insulin-dependent; GAD65, ICA antibodies"],["Type 2 (90%)","Insulin resistance + β-cell dysfunction","Adults, obese, family history, no ketosis"],["Gestational","Pregnancy hormones","Risk of later type 2"],["Monogenic","MODY (glucokinase, HNF1α)","Young, autosomal dominant"],["Secondary","Pancreatitis, haemochromatosis, Cushing, steroids","—"]]],
["Pathogenesis",["Type 1: genetic (HLA-DR3/DR4) + environmental (viruses) → T-cell–mediated insulitis → β-cell loss → absolute insulin deficiency","Type 2: obesity → FFAs, adipokines, inflammation → insulin resistance; β-cell dysfunction (glucotoxicity, lipotoxicity, amyloid) → relative insulin deficiency","Metabolic syndrome: central obesity, dyslipidaemia, hypertension"]],
["Morphology",["Pancreas: type 1 — insulitis, small islets, β-cell loss; type 2 — amyloid deposition in islets, mild β-cell reduction","Vessels: accelerated atherosclerosis, hyaline arteriolosclerosis","Kidney: GBM thickening, diffuse and nodular (Kimmelstiel–Wilson) glomerulosclerosis, papillary necrosis, pyelonephritis","Eye: retinopathy (microaneurysms, haemorrhages, exudates, neovascularisation), cataract, glaucoma","Nerves: peripheral symmetric sensorimotor neuropathy, autonomic neuropathy"]],
["Clinical features",[["Type 1","Type 2"],["Polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia, weight loss","Often asymptomatic; fatigue, infections"],["Diabetic ketoacidosis","Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state"],["Onset abrupt","Insidious, complications at diagnosis"]]]],
{i:"India has one of the largest populations with diabetes in the world; most have type 2 disease linked to urbanisation, diet and inactivity.",x:[["Mechanisms of complications",["Advanced glycation end products → cross-linking of proteins, RAGE-mediated inflammation","Protein kinase C activation → VEGF, TGF-β","Polyol pathway: glucose → sorbitol (aldose reductase) → osmotic damage (lens, nerves)","Hexosamine pathway"]],["Laboratory diagnosis",["Fasting and post-prandial glucose, OGTT","HbA1c (glycaemic control over 2–3 months)","Urine glucose and ketones; microalbuminuria","Islet autoantibodies, C-peptide (type 1 vs 2)"]],["Complications",["Acute: DKA, HHS, hypoglycaemia","Chronic macrovascular: CAD, stroke, peripheral vascular disease, gangrene","Chronic microvascular: retinopathy, nephropathy, neuropathy","Diabetic foot, infections (TB, mucormycosis, UTI)"]]],f:"Type 1: autoimmunity → β-cell loss → absolute insulin ↓\nType 2: obesity → insulin resistance + β-cell failure\n → hyperglycaemia → AGEs, PKC, polyol pathway\n ├ macrovascular: atherosclerosis → MI, stroke, gangrene\n └ microvascular: retina, kidney, nerves",c:"Diabetes damages every organ through chronic hyperglycaemia; early diagnosis, glycaemic, blood pressure and lipid control, and regular screening for complications prevent most morbidity."});

Z("path16",7,"p",["Osteosarcoma is the commonest primary malignant bone tumour, in which cancer cells directly make bone (osteoid).","A bone-making factory gone rogue near the growing ends of long bones in teenagers.",["Age 10–20 (second peak elderly with Paget disease, radiation).","Metaphysis of long bones; knee (distal femur, proximal tibia) commonest.","X-ray: sunburst pattern, Codman triangle.","RB and TP53 mutations; spreads by blood to lungs."],"Osteosarcoma = 'Teen knee, Sunburst, Codman, lungs'."],
"Osteosarcoma is a malignant mesenchymal tumour whose cancer cells produce osteoid or bone matrix. It is the most common primary malignant bone tumour, with a bimodal age distribution: adolescents (75%, during growth spurt) and older adults (secondary to Paget disease, radiation, bone infarcts). Associated genetic changes: RB1 (hereditary retinoblastoma), TP53 (Li–Fraumeni), CDKN2A. Usually arises in the metaphysis of long bones, mainly around the knee (distal femur, proximal tibia) and proximal humerus. Presents with progressive painful mass, sometimes pathological fracture. X-ray: destructive mixed lytic–blastic lesion with infiltrative margins, periosteal elevation forming Codman triangle and sunburst spiculation. Grossly gritty, grey-white, haemorrhagic, destroying cortex. Microscopy: pleomorphic malignant cells with lace-like neoplastic osteoid; may include cartilage. Spreads haematogenously, especially to lungs. Neoadjuvant chemotherapy and limb-salvage surgery give 60–70% survival.",
[["Features",["Peak 10–20 years; males","Metaphysis of distal femur, proximal tibia, proximal humerus","X-ray: sunburst, Codman triangle, mixed lytic–sclerotic","Micro: malignant osteoid laid down by anaplastic cells; ↑ ALP","Lung metastases"]],
["Bone tumours by site",[["Site","Tumour"],["Epiphysis","Giant cell tumour, chondroblastoma"],["Metaphysis","Osteosarcoma, osteochondroma"],["Diaphysis","Ewing sarcoma, myeloma"]]]],
{c:"Osteosarcoma should be suspected in a teenager with persistent knee pain and swelling; multimodal therapy has greatly improved survival."});

Z("path16",8,"p",["Giant cell tumour (osteoclastoma) is a locally aggressive bone tumour of young adults containing many osteoclast-like giant cells, usually at the end of long bones.","Many bone-eating giant cells hollowing out the end of the bone, making it look like soap bubbles on X-ray.",["Age 20–40 (after epiphyseal closure).","Epiphysis–metaphysis of long bones, knee commonest.","X-ray: eccentric lytic lesion with 'soap-bubble' appearance, extending to articular cartilage.","Neoplastic cells are mononuclear stromal cells expressing RANKL; recur after curettage; rarely metastasise to lung."],"GCT = 'Giant cells, Grown-up (20–40), Gnaws the epiphysis'."],
"Giant cell tumour of bone is a benign but locally aggressive neoplasm of young adults (20–40 years) arising in the epiphysis of long bones after growth plate closure, most commonly around the knee (distal femur, proximal tibia) and distal radius. The neoplastic cells are mononuclear osteoblast-precursor-like stromal cells expressing RANKL (H3F3A mutation), which recruit and activate numerous reactive osteoclast-like multinucleated giant cells. Clinically: pain, swelling, pathological fracture. X-ray: large, eccentric, purely lytic, expansile lesion extending to subchondral bone, with thin cortical shell ('soap bubble'). Grossly red-brown, haemorrhagic with cystic change. Microscopy: uniformly distributed giant cells (often 100 nuclei) amid sheets of oval mononuclear cells whose nuclei resemble those of the giant cells. Local recurrence after curettage is frequent (40–60%); 2–4% metastasise to lungs. Denosumab (anti-RANKL) is used in unresectable cases.",
[["Features",["Epiphysis of long bones; skeletally mature","Soap-bubble lytic lesion","Giant cells uniformly spread among mononuclear stromal cells","High recurrence, rare benign lung implants"]],
["Differential: other giant cell lesions",["Aneurysmal bone cyst","Brown tumour of hyperparathyroidism","Chondroblastoma","Giant cell reparative granuloma"]]],
{c:"GCT is benign but locally aggressive; complete excision with adjuvants reduces recurrence."});

Z("path16",9,"p",["Ewing's sarcoma is a highly malignant small round blue cell tumour of bone in children and teenagers, defined by the EWSR1–FLI1 gene fusion.","Small, primitive, fast-dividing cells packed tightly together like blue marbles, growing in the shaft of long bones.",["Age 5–20; diaphysis of long bones and pelvis.","t(11;22) EWSR1–FLI1 fusion; CD99 positive.","Mimics infection: fever, pain, raised ESR; X-ray 'onion-skin' periosteal reaction.","Histology: sheets of small round blue cells with glycogen (PAS+), Homer Wright rosettes."],"Ewing = '11;22, onion skin, CD99, diaphysis'."],
"Ewing sarcoma is a primitive malignant round cell tumour of bone and soft tissue, the second most common bone sarcoma in children, mostly 10–20 years, more often in males. Over 85% have t(11;22)(q24;q12) creating the EWSR1–FLI1 fusion transcription factor. It arises in the diaphysis of long bones (femur) and flat bones (pelvis). Clinically: painful, tender, warm swelling with fever, leukocytosis and raised ESR, mimicking osteomyelitis. X-ray: destructive lytic lesion with permeative margins and periosteal reaction producing layered 'onion-skin' appearance. Microscopy: sheets of uniform small round cells with scant clear cytoplasm rich in glycogen (PAS positive), occasional Homer Wright rosettes; CD99 (membranous) and FLI1/NKX2.2 positive. Highly aggressive with early metastases to lung and bone, but sensitive to chemotherapy and radiotherapy; 5-year survival 70–80% for localised disease.",
[["Small round blue cell tumours of childhood",[["Tumour","Marker"],["Ewing sarcoma","CD99, t(11;22)"],["Neuroblastoma","Neuron-specific enolase, ↑ VMA"],["Rhabdomyosarcoma","Desmin, myogenin"],["Lymphoma","CD45"],["Wilms tumour","WT1"],["Retinoblastoma","RB loss"]]]],
{c:"Ewing sarcoma mimics infection clinically; biopsy with CD99 and molecular testing confirms it, and combined therapy cures most localised cases."});

Z("path16",10,"p",["Lipoma is a common benign tumour of mature fat; liposarcoma is a rare malignant fat tumour of deep tissues in older adults.","Lipoma: a soft, well-wrapped ball of normal fat. Liposarcoma: a large deep fat tumour with abnormal immature fat cells (lipoblasts).",["Lipoma: commonest soft-tissue tumour; subcutaneous, soft, mobile, slipping edge; encapsulated mature adipocytes.","Liposarcoma: thigh and retroperitoneum, adults 50–60; lipoblasts (scalloped nuclei by vacuoles) diagnostic.","Types of liposarcoma: well-differentiated (MDM2 amplification), myxoid (t(12;16)), pleomorphic.","Lipomas do not turn into liposarcoma."],"Lipoma = superficial & simple; Liposarcoma = deep & lipoblasts."],
"Lipoma is the most common benign soft tissue tumour of adults, composed of mature adipocytes. It is usually a soft, mobile, painless, well-encapsulated subcutaneous mass on the trunk, neck and proximal limbs; variants include angiolipoma, spindle cell, pleomorphic and hibernoma. Liposarcoma is one of the most common sarcomas of adults (50–70 years), arising in deep soft tissues of the thigh and retroperitoneum. Subtypes: well-differentiated/atypical lipomatous tumour (MDM2 and CDK4 amplification, indolent), dedifferentiated, myxoid (FUS–DDIT3, t(12;16), plexiform capillaries, intermediate) and pleomorphic (aggressive). The diagnostic cell is the lipoblast — an immature fat cell with cytoplasmic lipid vacuoles that scallop the nucleus. Lipomas do not become liposarcomas.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Lipoma","Liposarcoma"],["Age","40–60","50–70"],["Site","Subcutaneous, trunk, neck","Deep: thigh, retroperitoneum"],["Size","Small (< 5 cm)","Large (> 10 cm)"],["Capsule","Thin, complete","Infiltrative"],["Histology","Mature adipocytes, uniform","Lipoblasts, atypia, myxoid stroma"],["Genetics","HMGA2 rearrangement","MDM2 amplification; t(12;16) in myxoid"],["Behaviour","Benign, cured by excision","Recurrence, metastasis (high grade)"]]]],
{c:"Large, deep, rapidly growing fatty masses should be suspected as liposarcoma and biopsied, while superficial soft mobile lumps are usually lipomas."});

Z("path16",11,"p",["Meningitis is inflammation of the meninges covering the brain and spinal cord, most often caused by bacteria (pyogenic), viruses (aseptic) or TB.","Like fire spreading in the thin wrappings around the brain, with pus or inflammatory fluid filling the CSF spaces.",["Pyogenic: neonates — E. coli, group B strep; children/adults — pneumococcus, meningococcus, H. influenzae.","CSF pyogenic: turbid, ↑ neutrophils, ↑ protein, ↓ glucose.","Tuberculous: basal exudate, cobweb clot, lymphocytes, very ↑ protein, ↓ glucose.","Viral: clear CSF, lymphocytes, normal glucose."],"CSF glucose low = bacteria or TB; normal = virus."],
"Meningitis is inflammation of the leptomeninges and CSF in the subarachnoid space, usually infective. Acute pyogenic (bacterial) meningitis: neonates — E. coli, group B streptococcus, Listeria; children and adults — Streptococcus pneumoniae, Neisseria meningitidis (epidemics, Waterhouse–Friderichsen syndrome), H. influenzae type b; elderly — pneumococcus, Listeria. Exudate of neutrophils over convexities (pneumococcus) or base fills the subarachnoid space; complications include ventriculitis, hydrocephalus, cerebral infarction and cranial nerve palsies. Aseptic (viral) meningitis is milder (enteroviruses), with lymphocytic CSF. Chronic tuberculous meningitis produces a gelatinous basal exudate, obliterative endarteritis causing infarcts, and hydrocephalus. Fungal (cryptococcal) meningitis occurs in AIDS. Clinical: fever, headache, neck rigidity, photophobia, altered sensorium, Kernig and Brudzinski signs.",
[["CSF findings",[["Feature","Normal","Pyogenic","Tuberculous","Viral"],["Appearance","Clear","Turbid","Clear/opalescent, cobweb","Clear"],["Cells /µL","0–5 lymphocytes","1000s neutrophils","100–500 lymphocytes","10–500 lymphocytes"],["Protein (mg/dL)","15–45","↑ (> 100)","↑↑ (100–500)","Mildly ↑"],["Glucose","50–80 (2/3 blood)","↓↓","↓","Normal"],["Organism","—","Gram stain, culture","AFB, CBNAAT","PCR"]]],
["Morphology",["Pyogenic: purulent exudate over convexities/base, congested vessels","TB: basal gelatinous exudate, tubercles, endarteritis, hydrocephalus","Cryptococcal: gelatinous, India ink positive capsule"]]],
{c:"CSF analysis is the key to diagnosing and differentiating meningitis; bacterial meningitis is an emergency needing immediate antibiotics."});

Z("path16",12,"p",["Meningioma is a usually benign tumour arising from arachnoid cap cells of the meninges, pressing on the brain rather than invading it.","A slow-growing lump attached to the brain's wrapping that pushes the brain aside like a stone in clay.",["Adults, women (progesterone receptors); NF2 association.","Dural attachment; convexity, falx, sphenoid ridge, olfactory groove.","Histology: whorls of meningothelial cells, psammoma bodies.","Mostly WHO grade 1; atypical and anaplastic forms recur."],"Meningioma = 'Middle-aged women, whorls + psammoma, dural tail'."],
"Meningiomas are predominantly benign tumours of adults arising from arachnoid meningothelial cells, usually attached to the dura. They are more common in women (progesterone receptors), and associated with NF2 (chromosome 22 loss) and prior radiation. Sites: parasagittal convexity, falx, sphenoid wing, olfactory groove, tentorium, spinal cord. They grow slowly, compressing but not invading brain, producing seizures, headache and focal deficits; they may cause hyperostosis of adjacent bone. Grossly well-circumscribed, rounded, firm, dura-based masses. Microscopy: syncytial (meningothelial), fibroblastic, transitional (whorls) and psammomatous patterns; EMA and progesterone receptor positive. WHO grade 1 (most), grade 2 atypical (increased mitoses, brain invasion), grade 3 anaplastic. MRI shows a 'dural tail'. Surgical excision is usually curative.",
[["Morphology",["Gross: round, encapsulated, dura-attached, gritty","Micro: whorls of cells, psammoma bodies, intranuclear inclusions","IHC: EMA, PR, SSTR2A"]],
["Grades",["Grade 1: benign variants","Grade 2: atypical — ≥ 4 mitoses/10 HPF, brain invasion","Grade 3: anaplastic — ≥ 20 mitoses/10 HPF, sarcoma-like"]]],
{c:"Most meningiomas are benign and curable by surgery; recurrence depends on grade and completeness of excision."});

Z("path16",13,"p",["Schwannoma is a benign tumour of Schwann cells (nerve sheath cells), commonly on the vestibular (VIII) nerve.","A lump growing on the side of a nerve like a bead on a string — the nerve can often be peeled away from it.",["Vestibular schwannoma (acoustic neuroma): cerebellopontine angle, hearing loss, tinnitus.","Bilateral vestibular schwannomas = NF2.","Histology: Antoni A (cellular, Verocay bodies) and Antoni B (loose, myxoid) areas.","S-100 strongly positive; encapsulated; rarely malignant."],"Schwannoma = 'Antoni A & B, Verocay bodies, S-100'."],
"Schwannomas are benign tumours of Schwann cells arising from peripheral, cranial or spinal nerve roots. Commonest intracranial site is the vestibular branch of the eighth nerve at the cerebellopontine angle (acoustic neuroma), causing unilateral sensorineural hearing loss, tinnitus and vertigo, later facial and trigeminal nerve involvement. Bilateral vestibular schwannomas indicate neurofibromatosis type 2 (merlin, chromosome 22). They are well-circumscribed, encapsulated masses attached eccentrically to the nerve, which can be separated. Microscopy: alternating Antoni A areas (dense cellular spindle cells with nuclear palisading forming Verocay bodies) and Antoni B areas (loose, hypocellular, myxoid), hyalinised vessels; strongly and diffusely S-100 and SOX10 positive. Malignant transformation is extremely rare (unlike neurofibroma in NF1).",
[["Schwannoma vs neurofibroma",[["Feature","Schwannoma","Neurofibroma"],["Cell","Schwann cells only","Mixed: Schwann, fibroblasts, perineurial cells"],["Relation to nerve","Eccentric, separable","Nerve incorporated within"],["Capsule","Present","Absent"],["Histology","Antoni A/B, Verocay bodies","Wavy 'shredded carrot' collagen, mast cells"],["Association","NF2","NF1"],["Malignant change","Very rare","Plexiform type can become MPNST"]]]],
{c:"Schwannomas are benign, encapsulated nerve sheath tumours removable while preserving the nerve; bilateral acoustic tumours point to NF2."});

Z("path16",14,"p",["A Pap smear is a simple test in which cells scraped from the cervix are stained and examined to detect precancerous and cancerous changes early.","A routine 'security camera' check of the cervix: catching suspicious cells before they become cancer.",["Sample from transformation zone using Ayre's spatula and cytobrush (or liquid-based cytology).","Fixed in 95% ethanol; stained by Papanicolaou stain (haematoxylin, OG-6, EA-50).","Reported by Bethesda system: NILM, ASC-US, LSIL, HSIL, SCC, AGC.","Screening from age 21–25, every 3 years (5 years with HPV co-testing)."],"Pap = 'Scrape, Fix wet, Stain, Bethesda report'."],
"The Papanicolaou (Pap) smear is a cytological screening test for cervical cancer and its precursors. Cells are collected from the ectocervix and endocervix (transformation zone) with an Ayre's spatula and endocervical brush, smeared on a slide and immediately wet-fixed in 95% ethyl alcohol (or collected in liquid-based medium). The Papanicolaou stain uses haematoxylin (nuclei), Orange G (keratin) and EA-50 (eosin, light green, Bismarck brown) for cytoplasm, producing pink superficial and blue-green intermediate/parabasal cells with crisp nuclear detail. Reporting uses the Bethesda system: specimen adequacy; negative for intraepithelial lesion or malignancy (NILM, including organisms such as Trichomonas, Candida, herpes, and reactive changes); epithelial cell abnormalities — squamous (ASC-US, ASC-H, LSIL, HSIL, squamous cell carcinoma) and glandular (AGC, AIS, adenocarcinoma). Uses: screening for CIN and carcinoma, hormonal evaluation, detection of infections. Limitations: false negatives from sampling errors.",
[["Procedure",["Patient not menstruating, no douching for 48 h","Visualise cervix with speculum; scrape transformation zone 360° with Ayre's spatula, endocervix with brush","Spread thinly, fix immediately in 95% alcohol (avoid air drying)","Stain with Papanicolaou stain"]],
["Bethesda categories",[["Category","Meaning"],["NILM","Normal / reactive / infection"],["ASC-US","Atypical squamous cells, undetermined significance"],["ASC-H","Atypical, cannot exclude HSIL"],["LSIL","HPV effect / CIN 1"],["HSIL","CIN 2–3"],["SCC","Invasive carcinoma"],["AGC / AIS","Glandular atypia / adenocarcinoma in situ"]]]],
{c:"Organised Pap smear screening has greatly reduced cervical cancer mortality wherever it has been implemented."});

Z("path16",15,"p",["FNAC (fine needle aspiration cytology) is a quick, cheap, minimally invasive test where cells are sucked out of a lump with a thin needle and examined under the microscope.","Like taking a small spoonful of soup to taste it instead of cooking a whole new pot (biopsy).",["Uses 22–25 gauge needle with syringe (with/without suction).","Common sites: thyroid, breast, lymph nodes, salivary glands, soft tissue; image-guided for deep organs.","Stains: Giemsa/MGG (air-dried), Pap and H&E (wet-fixed).","Advantages: OPD procedure, rapid, cheap, safe. Limitation: cannot assess tissue architecture or invasion."],"FNAC = 'Fast, Needle-thin, Affordable, Cells only'."],
"Fine needle aspiration cytology is the sampling of cells from a lesion using a fine needle (22–25 gauge) attached to a syringe, with or without negative pressure, followed by smearing and staining. It is used for palpable lumps (thyroid, breast, lymph nodes, salivary gland, soft tissue) and, with ultrasound or CT guidance, for deep lesions (liver, lung, pancreas, kidney). Smears are air-dried for Giemsa/May–Grünwald–Giemsa or wet-fixed for Papanicolaou and H&E staining; material can be used for cell blocks, immunocytochemistry, microbiology (AFB, culture) and molecular tests. Advantages: simple OPD procedure, quick, inexpensive, minimally invasive, minimal complications, repeatable. Limitations: inadequate samples, inability to assess architecture and invasion (e.g., follicular adenoma vs carcinoma, in situ vs invasive breast carcinoma), and need for experienced cytopathologists. Complications are rare: haematoma, infection, pneumothorax.",
[["Indications",["Thyroid nodules (Bethesda thyroid reporting)","Breast lumps (triple assessment)","Lymphadenopathy: TB, reactive, metastasis, lymphoma (needs biopsy for typing)","Salivary gland, soft tissue swellings","Deep lesions by image guidance"]],
["Advantages vs limitations",[["Advantages","Limitations"],["Quick, OPD, cheap","Cannot assess architecture/invasion"],["Minimal complications","Sampling errors, false negatives"],["Material for ICC, culture, molecular","Follicular thyroid lesions indeterminate"],["Repeatable, no anaesthesia","Needs trained cytopathologist"]]]],
{c:"FNAC is a first-line diagnostic tool for superficial and image-accessible lesions; doubtful cases need histopathological biopsy."});

Z("path16",16,"p",["Papillary and follicular carcinomas are the two well-differentiated thyroid cancers; papillary is the commonest and spreads by lymphatics, follicular spreads by blood.","Papillary cancer is recognised by its nuclei ('Orphan Annie eyes'); follicular cancer only by breaking out of its capsule or vessels.",["Papillary: 80–85%, radiation exposure, BRAF V600E/RET-PTC; ground-glass nuclei, grooves, inclusions, psammoma bodies; cervical nodes; excellent prognosis.","Follicular: 10–15%, iodine deficiency, RAS mutations; capsular or vascular invasion; metastasises to bone and lung.","FNAC diagnoses papillary but cannot distinguish follicular adenoma from carcinoma.","Medullary (C cells, calcitonin, amyloid, MEN2) and anaplastic (elderly, lethal) are other types."],"Papillary = 'Popular, Psammoma, Pseudo-inclusions, Positive nodes, Prognosis good'."],
"Thyroid carcinomas arise from follicular epithelium (papillary, follicular, anaplastic) or parafollicular C cells (medullary). Papillary carcinoma is the commonest (80–85%), occurring at 25–50 years, linked to ionising radiation; mutations: BRAF V600E, RET/PTC rearrangements. Grossly solitary or multifocal, may be cystic, with papillae. Diagnosis rests on nuclear features: optically clear 'ground-glass' (Orphan Annie eye) nuclei, nuclear grooves, intranuclear pseudo-inclusions, overlapping; papillae with fibrovascular cores and psammoma bodies. Spreads via lymphatics to cervical nodes; 10-year survival > 95%. Follicular carcinoma (10–15%) is associated with iodine deficiency and RAS or PAX8–PPARG mutations; it forms a solitary encapsulated nodule of microfollicles; diagnosis requires capsular and/or vascular invasion (cannot be made on FNAC); spreads haematogenously to lungs and bones; prognosis good with radioiodine.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Papillary","Follicular"],["Frequency","80–85%","10–15%"],["Risk","Radiation","Iodine deficiency"],["Genetics","BRAF, RET/PTC","RAS, PAX8–PPARG"],["Diagnostic features","Nuclear: ground glass, grooves, inclusions; psammoma bodies","Capsular/vascular invasion"],["Spread","Lymphatic (cervical nodes)","Blood (lung, bone)"],["FNAC","Diagnostic","Cannot distinguish adenoma"],["Prognosis","Excellent","Good"]]],
["Other thyroid carcinomas",["Medullary: C cells, calcitonin, amyloid stroma, RET mutation, MEN 2A/2B","Anaplastic: elderly, rapidly growing, TP53, almost uniformly fatal"]]],
{c:"Well-differentiated thyroid cancers have excellent outcomes after thyroidectomy and radioiodine; papillary is diagnosed by nuclear features, follicular by invasion."});

Z("path16",17,"p",["Adrenal disorders: Cushing syndrome is excess cortisol; pheochromocytoma is a catecholamine-secreting tumour of the adrenal medulla; Addison disease is failure of the adrenal cortex.","The adrenal is a two-part factory: the outer cortex makes steroids (too much = Cushing, too little = Addison), the inner medulla makes adrenaline (tumour = pheochromocytoma).",["Cushing: commonest cause is steroid therapy; endogenous — pituitary adenoma (Cushing disease), adrenal tumour, ectopic ACTH (small cell lung cancer).","Cushing features: moon face, buffalo hump, central obesity, purple striae, hypertension, hyperglycaemia, osteoporosis.","Pheochromocytoma 'rule of 10s'; paroxysmal hypertension, headache, sweating, palpitations; ↑ urinary VMA/metanephrines.","Addison: autoimmune (commonest), TB in India; hyperpigmentation, hypotension, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia."],"Pheo = 'Rule of 10s'; Addison = 'Darkening + low BP + low Na, high K'."],
"Cushing syndrome results from prolonged exposure to excess glucocorticoids: exogenous (iatrogenic steroids, commonest), pituitary ACTH-secreting adenoma (Cushing disease, bilateral adrenal hyperplasia), primary adrenal adenoma or carcinoma (cortical atrophy of the other gland) and ectopic ACTH (small cell lung carcinoma). Features: central obesity, moon facies, buffalo hump, purple striae, hypertension, glucose intolerance, osteoporosis, proximal myopathy, hirsutism, immunosuppression; diagnosis by raised 24-h urinary free cortisol and dexamethasone suppression testing. Pheochromocytoma is a catecholamine-secreting tumour of chromaffin cells, following the 'rule of 10s' (10% bilateral, extra-adrenal, malignant, familial — now ~25–30% hereditary with MEN2, VHL, NF1, SDH); presents with paroxysmal or sustained hypertension, headache, palpitations, sweating; tumour cells in nests (zellballen) with sustentacular cells, chromaffin positive; raised urinary VMA and metanephrines. Addison disease (primary chronic adrenocortical insufficiency) is caused by autoimmune adrenalitis, tuberculosis (important in India), AIDS, metastases, amyloidosis; destruction of > 90% of cortex leads to weakness, hypotension, hyperpigmentation (↑ ACTH/MSH), hyponatraemia and hyperkalaemia; acute adrenal crisis can be fatal.",
[["Summary",[["Disorder","Cause","Key features","Tests"],["Cushing syndrome","Steroids, pituitary adenoma, adrenal tumour, ectopic ACTH","Central obesity, striae, hypertension, hyperglycaemia","Urinary free cortisol, dexamethasone suppression, ACTH"],["Pheochromocytoma","Chromaffin cell tumour","Episodic hypertension, sweating, palpitations","Urinary/plasma metanephrines, VMA"],["Addison disease","Autoimmune, TB, metastases","Pigmentation, hypotension, ↓Na⁺, ↑K⁺","Low cortisol, high ACTH, ACTH stimulation test"]]],
["Pathology",["Cushing: bilateral nodular hyperplasia (ACTH) or adenoma with contralateral atrophy; Crooke hyaline change in pituitary","Pheochromocytoma: brown-tan mass, zellballen, salt-and-pepper chromatin, chromogranin positive","Addison: small atrophic adrenals (autoimmune) or caseating TB destruction"]]],
{c:"Adrenal disorders are recognised by distinctive clinical syndromes; biochemical confirmation precedes imaging to localise the lesion."});

Z("path16",18,"p",["A pituitary adenoma is a benign tumour of the anterior pituitary that may over-secrete a hormone or press on surrounding structures.","A lump in a small bony cup (sella) that either shouts too loudly (hormone excess) or squeezes its neighbours (optic chiasm).",["Commonest: prolactinoma — galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea, infertility; treated with cabergoline.","GH adenoma: gigantism (children), acromegaly (adults).","ACTH adenoma: Cushing disease.","Mass effect: bitemporal hemianopia (optic chiasm), headache, hypopituitarism."],"Pituitary = 'Prolactin commonest; Bitemporal blindness from pressure'."],
"Pituitary adenomas are benign tumours of the anterior pituitary, the commonest cause of hyperpituitarism. They are classified as functional or non-functional, and by size into microadenomas (< 1 cm) and macroadenomas (≥ 1 cm). Types by hormone: prolactinoma (commonest; amenorrhoea, galactorrhoea, infertility, loss of libido), somatotroph adenoma (GH; gigantism before epiphyseal fusion, acromegaly after; GNAS mutation), corticotroph adenoma (ACTH; Cushing disease), gonadotroph and non-functioning adenomas (mass effects), thyrotroph (rare). Mass effects: headache, bitemporal hemianopia from optic chiasm compression, cranial nerve palsies, raised ICP and hypopituitarism by compression of normal gland. Pituitary apoplexy is sudden haemorrhage into an adenoma. Microscopy: sheets of uniform polygonal cells with loss of the normal mixed acinar pattern (reticulin lost); IHC identifies hormone.",
[["Types",[["Adenoma","Hormone","Clinical"],["Lactotroph (prolactinoma)","Prolactin","Galactorrhoea, amenorrhoea, infertility"],["Somatotroph","GH","Gigantism, acromegaly, diabetes"],["Corticotroph","ACTH","Cushing disease"],["Gonadotroph / null cell","Non-functioning","Mass effects, hypopituitarism"],["Thyrotroph","TSH","Hyperthyroidism (rare)"]]],
["Morphology",["Soft well-circumscribed mass in sella; may erode sella or invade sinuses","Monomorphic cells, loss of reticulin network","Hormone IHC; transcription factors (PIT1, TPIT, SF1)"]]],
{c:"Pituitary adenomas present with hormone excess or mass effects; MRI and hormonal assays guide treatment with drugs, transsphenoidal surgery or radiotherapy."});

Z("path16",19,"p",["Osteomyelitis is infection of bone and marrow, most often by Staphylococcus aureus, causing bone death (sequestrum) and new bone formation (involucrum).","Infection traps itself inside the rigid bone; pressure kills the bone (sequestrum) and the body builds a new shell around it (involucrum).",["Haematogenous in children: metaphysis of long bones (slow sinusoidal flow).","Organisms: S. aureus (commonest); Salmonella in sickle cell; Pseudomonas in IV drug users and foot puncture; TB (Pott's spine).","Sequestrum = dead bone; involucrum = new periosteal bone; cloaca = opening.","Chronic: Brodie abscess, sinus tracts, amyloidosis, squamous carcinoma in sinus."],"Osteomyelitis 'S-I-C': Sequestrum, Involucrum, Cloaca."],
"Osteomyelitis is inflammation of bone and marrow, almost always due to infection. Pyogenic osteomyelitis is usually bacterial: haematogenous spread (children — metaphysis of long bones; adults — vertebrae), direct contamination (open fracture, surgery) or contiguous spread (diabetic foot). Staphylococcus aureus causes 80–90%; others: E. coli and group B streptococci in neonates, Salmonella in sickle cell disease, Pseudomonas in IV drug users. Acute stage: neutrophilic infiltration, raised intraosseous pressure, ischaemic necrosis of bone forming a sequestrum; pus spreads through Haversian canals to lift periosteum; new reactive bone forms a sleeve (involucrum); openings in it are cloacae. Chronic stage: persistent infection, sinus tracts, Brodie abscess (localised abscess surrounded by sclerotic bone), sclerosing osteomyelitis of Garré. Complications: pathological fracture, septicaemia, amyloidosis, squamous cell carcinoma in sinus tract, growth disturbance. Tuberculous osteomyelitis commonly affects the spine (Pott disease) with caseous destruction and cold abscess.",
[["Stages",["Acute: hyperaemia, neutrophils, oedema, ↑ pressure","Subacute: bone necrosis → sequestrum; subperiosteal abscess","Chronic: involucrum, cloacae, sinus tracts, fibrosis, Brodie abscess"]],
["Complications",["Pathological fracture","Septicaemia, septic arthritis (infants)","Chronic draining sinus → squamous carcinoma","Secondary amyloidosis","Growth plate damage → limb shortening"]],
["Tuberculous osteomyelitis (Pott spine)",["Thoracolumbar vertebrae, disc involvement","Vertebral collapse → kyphosis (gibbus)","Psoas cold abscess; paraplegia"]]],
{c:"Early antibiotics prevent bone necrosis; once sequestra form, surgical debridement is needed to eradicate chronic osteomyelitis."});

Z("path16",20,"p",["Osteoporosis is thin, porous bone with normal mineral; rickets (children) and osteomalacia (adults) are soft bone due to poor mineralisation (vitamin D lack); Paget disease is chaotic, overactive bone remodelling.","Osteoporosis: a building with fewer bricks. Osteomalacia: bricks without hardened cement. Paget: workers tearing down and rebuilding in a frenzy, leaving a messy mosaic.",["Osteoporosis: postmenopausal and senile; vertebral, hip, wrist fractures; DEXA T-score ≤ −2.5.","Rickets: bowed legs, rickety rosary, craniotabes, widened wrists; ↓ Ca/PO4, ↑ ALP.","Osteomalacia: bone pain, Looser zones (pseudofractures).","Paget disease: elderly, thick enlarged bones, mosaic pattern, very high ALP, osteosarcoma risk."],"Porosis = less bone; Malacia = soft bone; Paget = mosaic bone + high ALP."],
"Osteoporosis is reduced bone mass with normal mineralisation, leading to fragility fractures. Primary osteoporosis is postmenopausal (oestrogen deficiency → ↑ RANKL, osteoclast activity) or senile (reduced osteoblast function); secondary causes include steroids, hyperthyroidism, hyperparathyroidism, immobilisation and malabsorption. Trabecular bone thins; fractures of vertebrae (kyphosis, height loss), hip and distal radius occur; DEXA T-score ≤ −2.5 defines it; serum Ca, PO4 and ALP are normal. Rickets (children) and osteomalacia (adults) are defective mineralisation of osteoid due to vitamin D deficiency (poor sunlight, diet, malabsorption, renal or liver disease) or hypophosphataemia. Rickets: craniotabes, frontal bossing, rachitic rosary, Harrison sulcus, widened wrists, bow legs, delayed dentition; X-ray cupping and fraying of metaphysis. Osteomalacia: bone pain, proximal myopathy, Looser zones. Lab: low/normal Ca, low phosphate, high ALP and PTH, low 25(OH)D; histology shows wide osteoid seams. Paget disease (osteitis deformans) is a localised disorder of excessive, disorganised bone remodelling in elderly people (osteolytic, mixed and sclerotic phases), producing thick, weak bones with mosaic pattern of cement lines; affects skull, pelvis, spine, femur; causes bone pain, deformity, deafness, high-output heart failure, very high ALP, and risk of osteosarcoma.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Osteoporosis","Osteomalacia/rickets","Paget disease"],["Defect","↓ Bone mass","↓ Mineralisation","Disordered remodelling"],["Serum Ca","Normal","↓/N","Normal"],["Serum PO4","Normal","↓","Normal"],["ALP","Normal","↑","↑↑↑"],["PTH","Normal","↑","Normal"],["Histology","Thin trabeculae","Wide osteoid seams","Mosaic cement lines"],["X-ray","Osteopenia, fractures","Looser zones; cupping (rickets)","Thick cortex, coarse trabeculae"]]]],
{c:"Bone biochemistry (Ca, PO4, ALP, PTH, vitamin D) distinguishes these metabolic bone diseases and guides specific therapy."});

Z("path16",21,"p",["Gout is arthritis caused by urate crystals deposited in joints; rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune disease where the joint lining (synovium) becomes inflamed and destroys cartilage and bone.","Gout: sharp needle crystals jabbing the joint. RA: the joint lining turning into an invading carpet (pannus) that eats cartilage.",["Gout: hyperuricaemia; first MTP joint (podagra); negatively birefringent needle crystals; tophi.","RA: women 30–50; symmetrical small joints of hands (MCP, PIP), morning stiffness > 1 h.","RA pathology: synovial hyperplasia, lymphoid follicles, pannus, ankylosis; rheumatoid nodules.","RA serology: rheumatoid factor, anti-CCP (more specific)."],"Gout = Great toe, needle crystals; RA = Pannus, anti-CCP, symmetrical small joints."],
"Gout is a disorder of hyperuricaemia (overproduction or underexcretion of uric acid) with deposition of monosodium urate crystals in joints and tissues. Primary gout (90%) is idiopathic or due to enzyme defects; secondary gout results from increased cell turnover (leukaemia, chemotherapy), renal failure, diuretics, alcohol, high-purine diet. Acute arthritis results from crystal-induced inflammation via the NLRP3 inflammasome and IL-1; classically the first metatarsophalangeal joint (podagra). Chronic tophaceous gout produces tophi (crystal aggregates surrounded by foreign body giant cells and macrophages), joint destruction and urate nephropathy/stones. Joint fluid shows needle-shaped negatively birefringent crystals. Rheumatoid arthritis is a chronic systemic autoimmune disease primarily affecting synovial joints symmetrically (MCP, PIP, wrists). Genetic (HLA-DRB1 shared epitope, PTPN22) and environmental factors (smoking — citrullination) lead to CD4 Th1/Th17 responses, B cells producing RF and anti-CCP antibodies, and cytokines (TNF, IL-6). Synovium becomes hyperplastic and villous with lymphoid follicles, plasma cells, neovascularisation and fibrin; the pannus erodes cartilage and bone, causing fibrous and bony ankylosis and deformities (ulnar deviation, swan-neck, boutonnière). Extra-articular: rheumatoid nodules (fibrinoid necrosis with palisading histiocytes), vasculitis, lung fibrosis, anaemia, amyloidosis.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Gout","Rheumatoid arthritis"],["Cause","Urate crystals","Autoimmunity"],["Patient","Men > 40","Women 30–50"],["Joints","Monoarticular, 1st MTP","Symmetrical polyarthritis of small joints"],["Pathology","Crystal-induced inflammation, tophi","Pannus, synovial hyperplasia, lymphoid follicles"],["Lab","↑ Serum urate; needle crystals in fluid","RF, anti-CCP, ↑ ESR/CRP"],["Extra-articular","Tophi, renal stones, urate nephropathy","Nodules, vasculitis, lung fibrosis, amyloid"]]]],
{c:"Crystal analysis of joint fluid diagnoses gout, while serology and symmetrical small-joint involvement define RA; both need early treatment to prevent joint destruction."});

Z("path16",22,"p",["CSF is made in the choroid plexus, flows through the ventricles into the subarachnoid space and is absorbed by arachnoid villi; gliomas are brain tumours arising from glial (support) cells.","CSF is like water flowing through a system of rooms and corridors; a block anywhere causes flooding (hydrocephalus). Gliomas are weeds growing among the brain's support cells.",["CSF: ~500 mL/day; lateral ventricles → foramen of Monro → 3rd ventricle → aqueduct → 4th ventricle → foramina of Luschka/Magendie → subarachnoid space → arachnoid villi.","Obstruction → non-communicating hydrocephalus; poor absorption → communicating.","Gliomas: astrocytoma (glioblastoma — grade 4, pseudopalisading necrosis), oligodendroglioma ('fried egg' cells, 1p/19q), ependymoma (children, 4th ventricle, perivascular pseudorosettes).","Adult tumours mostly supratentorial; childhood tumours infratentorial."],"Glioblastoma = 'Pseudopalisading necrosis + butterfly across corpus callosum'."],
"Cerebrospinal fluid is formed mainly by the choroid plexuses (about 500 mL/day, 150 mL volume) and circulates from the lateral ventricles through the interventricular foramina to the third ventricle, cerebral aqueduct, fourth ventricle and, via the foramina of Luschka and Magendie, into the subarachnoid space, to be absorbed by arachnoid granulations into the dural venous sinuses. Obstruction within the ventricular system causes non-communicating hydrocephalus; impaired absorption causes communicating hydrocephalus. Gliomas are the most common primary brain tumours, arising from astrocytes, oligodendrocytes and ependymal cells. WHO CNS 5 integrates histology and molecular markers: adult diffuse gliomas — astrocytoma IDH-mutant (grades 2–4), oligodendroglioma IDH-mutant and 1p/19q-codeleted, and glioblastoma IDH-wildtype (grade 4, commonest and most aggressive: necrosis with pseudopalisading, microvascular proliferation, crosses corpus callosum as 'butterfly glioma'). Paediatric types include pilocytic astrocytoma (cerebellum, Rosenthal fibres, BRAF fusion, good prognosis) and ependymoma (fourth ventricle, perivascular pseudorosettes). Medulloblastoma is an embryonal tumour of the cerebellum in children.",
[["CSF circulation",["Choroid plexus → lateral ventricles → foramen of Monro → third ventricle → aqueduct of Sylvius → fourth ventricle → Luschka/Magendie → subarachnoid space → arachnoid villi → superior sagittal sinus"]],
["Gliomas",[["Tumour","Age/site","Features"],["Pilocytic astrocytoma (grade 1)","Children, cerebellum","Cystic, Rosenthal fibres, BRAF; excellent prognosis"],["Diffuse astrocytoma IDH-mutant (2–4)","Young adults, cerebral hemispheres","Infiltrative, progresses"],["Glioblastoma IDH-wildtype (4)","Older adults, hemispheres","Necrosis with pseudopalisading, microvascular proliferation; survival ~15 months"],["Oligodendroglioma","Adults, frontal lobe","Fried-egg cells, chicken-wire vessels, calcification; 1p/19q codeletion; chemosensitive"],["Ependymoma","Children 4th ventricle; adults spinal cord","Perivascular pseudorosettes; hydrocephalus"]]]],
{c:"Understanding CSF pathways explains hydrocephalus; integrated histological and molecular classification of gliomas now determines prognosis and therapy."});

Z("path16",23,"p",["Retinoblastoma is the commonest eye cancer of children; uveal (ocular) melanoma is the commonest primary eye cancer of adults.","Retinoblastoma: a white glow in a child's pupil. Ocular melanoma: a pigmented mass behind an adult's eye, spreading through blood to the liver.",["Retinoblastoma: RB gene two-hit, leukocoria, Flexner–Wintersteiner rosettes.","Uveal melanoma: choroid commonest; GNAQ/GNA11 mutations (not BRAF).","Uveal melanoma spreads haematogenously, especially to the liver (no lymphatics in the eye).","Spindle cell type better prognosis than epithelioid."],"Child eye = Retinoblastoma (white reflex); Adult eye = Uveal melanoma (liver metastasis)."],
"Retinoblastoma is the most common primary intraocular malignancy of childhood, caused by biallelic loss of the RB1 gene; hereditary cases are bilateral and early. It presents with leukocoria, strabismus, glaucoma and proptosis. Microscopy: small round blue cells with necrosis and calcification, Flexner–Wintersteiner and Homer Wright rosettes and fleurettes. Spread occurs through the optic nerve to the brain and by blood. Uveal melanoma is the most common primary intraocular malignancy of adults (50–70 years), arising from melanocytes of the choroid (commonest), ciliary body or iris. Mutations involve GNAQ/GNA11 and BAP1 (not BRAF). It forms a mushroom-shaped mass after rupture of Bruch membrane, causing retinal detachment and visual loss. Cell types: spindle A and B (better prognosis) and epithelioid (worse). As the eye lacks lymphatics, spread is haematogenous, with a striking predilection for the liver, sometimes decades later.",
[["Comparison",[["Feature","Retinoblastoma","Uveal melanoma"],["Age","< 5 years","50–70 years"],["Origin","Retinal neuroepithelium","Uveal melanocytes (choroid)"],["Genetics","RB1","GNAQ/GNA11, BAP1, monosomy 3"],["Presentation","Leukocoria, squint","Visual loss, retinal detachment"],["Histology","Small blue cells, rosettes","Spindle and epithelioid melanocytes"],["Spread","Optic nerve, CNS, marrow","Blood to liver"]]]],
{c:"Both are major ocular cancers: retinoblastoma is curable when detected early by the white reflex, while uveal melanoma's hepatic spread determines survival."});
